ale ferro-cement 'leaves' over glass, run out on slender white columns as a porch all the way round, gardens let into the plan, the Treasure House (the storerooms) as a grey-sided second floor over the middle; live oaks along Sul Ross and Branard, grey bungalows of the neighbourhood. Footprint and street positions from the ground file (about 136 x 36 m, long east-west); the rest from the Menil's and published descriptions. Refined 10 Oct 2026 (no photographs; three rounds): the roof is now a single pale leaf plane (leaves 1.4 m wide at 2.8 m, the voxel's grain) instead of a checkered one; the Treasure House is a grey box with a darker glazed ribbon and a concrete cap, no longer white-finned; the oaks are broader umbrella crowns. Still guessed: number and places of the gardens, the entrance position, the leaves' direction and curvature (drawn as flat east-west rows), heights (about 7 m; 13.7 m at the Treasure House)."}